Every leaderboard comparison assumes that benchmark items measure the same construct across model generations---an assumption no one tests. We apply differential item functioning (DIF) analysis from educational measurement to test it. Analyzing 5,227 models across six METABENCH benchmarks, we find that 31\% of MMLU items function differently between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts---50$\times$ above random baselines---yet aggregate rankings remain near-perfect ($\rho \approx 0.997$). The instability is diagnostic, not destructive: base and instruct models exhibit opposite DIF directions, revealing structured capability shifts invisible to standard scoring. DIF flags are near-independent of web-overlap contamination labels, establishing differential functioning as a complementary diagnostic dimension that existing detectors miss entirely. We validate detection power through semi-synthetic injection with cross-subject matching that guarantees zero false-positive inflation, and propose an actionable audit protocol for benchmark maintainers\footnote{Code and data: \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/irt_equated_bench_regen-2431/}.}. LLM benchmarks need psychometric monitoring, not just score reporting.
\end{abstract}

